@*
    Chapter metadata: the block under a `##` heading, drawn as part of that
    heading.

    A chapter's record describes the chapter under it, and the heading is where a
    reader is looking when they ask what it says — so the two are one thing on the
    screen. The heading becomes the record's headline and the status takes the
    right edge of the heading's line; the rest of the block sits underneath,
    quieter, close enough to be taken in with the same glance. All of that is
    MetadataView's, and this component is the shape rather than the drawing: a
    record that has a heading to fold into.

    Which is why the heading is required here and optional there. MetadataView
    draws a record with a heading, without one, or with nothing but a status,
    because a record turns up in all three shapes. This is the one where the pair
    is the point, and a chapter component handed no heading would be the base
    component under a name that promises otherwise.

    It exists because the pairing was assembled at a call site. MarkdownView
    decides which fence belongs to which heading — that is the reading, and it
    stays there — and then had the whole of the chapter shape written out inline,
    the file shape written out again in FileView's header, and nothing naming
    either. Two call sites for two shapes is two things to keep in step; MetadataFileView
    beside this one is the other half of that.

    Nothing here draws markup of its own, and that is deliberate rather than
    unfinished. A wrapper element would put a div between the heading and the
    document that contains it, and the record's own layout — one flex row, the
    status pushed over by an auto margin — is measured against the heading's box.
    What this file adds is a name, a required heading, and one place for the
    chapter shape's decisions to be made when there is a next one.

    Empty in, nothing out, exactly as the record behaves: a fence that states
    nothing renders no element at all. A host that has a heading to draw whether
    or not there is a record draws it itself in that case — see MarkdownView,
    which pairs a heading with a fence and renders the heading alone when there is
    none. That is the difference from MetadataFileView, which takes the heading
    both ways: a chapter heading is a block in the document either way, and a
    file's name is only in the header because the record put it there.
*@

<MetadataView Metadata="@Metadata"
              Vocabulary="@Vocabulary"
              ShowFields="@ShowFields"
              OnStatusChanged="@OnStatusChanged"
              OnNavigate="@OnNavigate"
              HrefFor="@HrefFor"
              AriaLabel="@AriaLabel"
              CssClass="@CssClass"
              TestId="@TestId"
              Heading="@Heading" />

@code {
    /// <summary>The chapter's record, as the fence under its heading parsed.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public MetadataRecord Metadata { get; set; } = MetadataRecord.Empty;

    /// <summary>The heading this record belongs to, drawn as the record's
    /// headline. Required: the pairing is what this component is.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public RenderFragment? Heading { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The status words this chapter's surface allows, which is what
    /// turns the status from a word into a value out of a list. See
    /// <see cref="MetadataView.Vocabulary"/> — without one the status still shows,
    /// it is just not judged. A knowledge surface passes
    /// <c>KnowledgeStatus.Vocabulary(folder)</c>.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public MetadataStatusVocabulary Vocabulary { get; set; } = MetadataStatusVocabulary.None;

    /// <summary>Raised with the status a reader picked. A chapter's status is its
    /// own: a host wires this per chapter, and what it does with it — writing the
    /// fence back, or nothing — is the host's.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<string?> OnStatusChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Raised with the reference a reader activated.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<KnowledgeReference> OnNavigate { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Resolves a reference to a link target; null leaves that reference
    /// as text rather than a broken link.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public Func<KnowledgeReference, string?>? HrefFor { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether everything but the status is drawn. On by default, off for
    /// a surface showing the file itself, where those fields are the fence read
    /// back as rows.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool ShowFields { get; set; } = true;

    /// <summary>The group's name for a screen reader. Defaulted to the record's own
    /// default rather than to something this component prefers: a chapter in a
    /// document has always announced itself this way, and a new word here would
    /// rename every record in the product.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string AriaLabel { get; set; } = "Knowledge metadata";

    [Parameter]
    public string? CssClass { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? TestId { get; set; }
}
